Tue Sep 22 2026 (UTC) — WP1 pre-implementation measurement: durability cost, write counts,
and the platform guarantee the requirement's wording overstates.

Why this was measured before any code was written
-------------------------------------------------
WP1 obligation 2 asks to "flush file contents before replacement and use platform-supported
directory durability handling. Surface unsupported guarantees explicitly." The spec's draft
argued from inference: an unmeasured per-call flush cost multiplied by an assumed 3 writes per
mutation, set against an update-visibility gate recorded at 1956.6 ms / 2000 ms. That argument
concluded the change "can fail a gate that is already 43 ms from failing".

That was inference, not evidence, and spec §4 forbids exactly that shape. Two commands replaced
both guesses. The drafted alarm was NOT borne out and is withdrawn in §6.3.

1. Per-write cost — probe replicating packages/memory/src/atomic.ts:16-21 verbatim
   (mkdir, writeFileSync(tmp), renameSync) as `baseline`, and the spec §9 item 1 shape as
   `durable` (openSync tmp, write, fsyncSync fd, close, rename, openSync dir, fsyncSync dir,
   close). 300 iterations each, same directory, same 2 KB payload, APFS, darwin 25.6:

     baseline  p50 0.1183 ms   p95 0.1933 ms
     durable   p50 9.9374 ms   p95 11.0917 ms
     overhead  p50 +9.82 ms    p95 +10.90 ms  (58x)

2. Writes per mutation, counted on a real mutation. `node --require` preload wrapping
   fs.renameSync / fsyncSync / appendFileSync / writeFileSync, then a real
   `crib memory observe --kind fact --subject probe:*-total --evidence <source-quote>` on a
   scratch repo, ADMITTED to local trust. Steady state, identical across three runs:

     WRITE-COUNTS {"renameSync":12,"fsyncSync":0,"appendFileSync":1,"writeFileSync":13}

   Same probe over the whole of `crib init`: 22 renameSync, 36 writeFileSync, fsyncSync 0.
   That is an INDEPENDENT confirmation of the spec's tree-wide "no fsync anywhere" claim,
   observed rather than re-asserted.

Arithmetic: ~12 flushed writes x 10.90 ms p95  =  ~130 ms p95 added per mutation ~ 6.5% of the
2000 ms update-visibility budget. Affordable. Group-commit is probably unnecessary. The gate must
still be re-measured WITH the change in place, because 1956.6 ms is a different code path
(watch -> queryable, dominated by index/graph rebuild) than the mutation path measured here.

3. The finding that matters more — the platform guarantee gap.
   `man 2 fsync` on this machine, verbatim:

     Note that while fsync() will flush all data from the host to the drive (i.e. the "permanent
     storage device"), the drive itself may not physically write the data to the platters for
     quite some time and it may be written in an out-of-order sequence.
     ... if the drive loses power or the OS crashes, the application may find that only some or
     none of their data was written.
     ... Mac OS X provides the F_FULLFSYNC fcntl. The F_FULLFSYNC fcntl asks the drive to flush
     all buffered data to permanent storage. Applications, such as databases, that require a
     strict ordering of writes should use F_FULLFSYNC ...

   Consequences, recorded as spec defect D1-g:
   - On darwin, fsyncSync is a host-to-device flush, NOT a platter flush. Adding it does not
     deliver power-loss durability, which is what a reader of "flush file contents before
     replacement" would reasonably assume.
   - Node core exposes no F_FULLFSYNC. Obtaining it needs an fcntl binding, i.e. a native
     dependency, which this project should not take for this.
   - Therefore the capability surfaced by spec §9 item 2 must be THREE-valued, not two:
     { fileFlush, dirFlush, powerLossDurable }. "fileFlush: true" alone is misleading.
   - The honest claim becomes: an acknowledged write survives a PROCESS crash and is ordered on
     the device. Strictly stronger than today's page-cache acknowledgement; strictly weaker than
     power-loss durability. Spec §4 requires exactly that separation and §11.1 names the tests
     as flush-ordering tests, never as power-loss guarantees.

One probe error, recorded rather than quietly fixed
---------------------------------------------------
The first directory-flush probe reported `dirFlushSupported: false, ENOENT`. That was the probe's
OWN bug: it opened the directory before anything had created it (the mkdir happened inside the
baseline writer, as a side effect). Directory flush IS supported here: re-run with the directory
created first, `openSync(dir,'r')` + `fsyncSync` returns 0. Recorded because a probe bug that
reads as a platform limitation would have gone straight into the "surface unsupported
guarantees" work as a false fact.

